  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Saturday)\nIssued at 1206 AM CDT SUN SEP 6 2026\n\nAt Midnight, an upper-level ridge is stretched from Northern \nMexico into the Central Plains, maintaining weak northeasterly \nflow over Central Alabama. In the lower levels, weak surface \nridging persists over the Western Atlantic and into the Gulf. \nConvection from earlier has left several boundaries across the \narea. With northerly flow aloft ushering scattered showers and \nstorms into our northern counties, we will need to monitor for any\nincreasing convective trends. Short-term model guidance is \ninconsistent, ranging from robust coverage to minimal development \nthrough sunrise. Consequently, the forecast for the next 6 to 10 \nhours will be adjusted based on radar trends and boundary \ncollisions.\n\nConfidence is higher that a surface boundary will slide into the\narea later today, bringing another round of showers and storms.\nThere is a medium chance of convection, particularly along and\nsouth of this boundary. With lingering heat, any convection along\nthe boundary will have the potential to become strong to\nmarginally severe, especially during the afternoon and evening\nhours. A Level 1 out of 5 risk for severe storms remains through\nthe evening, generally across the southern half of the region,\nwith gusty winds as the primary threat. We will also monitor for\nany training or redevelopment over the same areas, which could\nlead to localized flooding.\n\nMonday into Tuesday, the upper ridge expands eastward across the\nCONUS. The best chances for convection will remain across the\nsouthern portions of the area, closer to the surface boundary and\nany upper disturbances moving westward around the base of the\nridge. Guidance suggests a breakdown of the upper ridge by mid-\nweek as large-scale troughing develops along the Mississippi\nRiver Valley out of Canada. Toward late week, northwest flow\nacross the Central U.S. should help push another front toward the\nSoutheast.\n\nThe approaching surface boundary should lower temperatures\nslightly and introduce drier air. While conditions will remain\nhot, they should stay just below our heat advisory criteria for\nMonday and Tuesday.\n\n16",
